---
name: object-var-member-scope
description: "a var member of an object belongs to that object, not to the top level"
backends: [int, js, jvm, v2]
---

# A `var` member of an `object` is scoped to the object

`v1/runtime/backend/interpreter/BUGS.md` `object-var-member-assignment-writes-a-top-level-global`.
Assigning to a `var` member from inside the object's own method wrote a TOP-LEVEL global of the same
bare name instead of the member, so the object's field never changed and an unrelated variable of
that name was silently overwritten.

Measured against the JVM oracle before the fix:

    lane    n     O.n
    jvm     100   8      the two are separate
    int     8     7      exactly backwards
    native  101   7      same class, on both fronts
    js      Error: Method not found: n

The shadowing block is the point of the case. A test with only `object O { var n }` and no
top-level `n` would pass on a lane that puts the member in the global scope — the object would
appear to work while quietly owning a name it does not own. Only a same-named outer `var` separates
"the member was updated" from "a global was updated and happened to be read back".

The last block guards the opposite mistake: two objects with the same member name must not share
one cell.

```scalascript
object Counter:
  var n: Int = 0
  def bump(): Unit = n = n + 1
  def value(): Int = n

object Other:
  var n: Int = 50
  def value(): Int = n

var n: Int = 100

def main() =
  // the object's own method reads and writes its own field
  println(Counter.value())
  Counter.bump()
  Counter.bump()
  println(Counter.value())

  // and the field is visible from outside, with the same answer
  println(Counter.n)

  // the top-level var of the same name is untouched
  println(n)

  // two objects with the same member name do not share a cell
  println(Other.n)
  println(Other.value())

  // a list-valued member, the shape this was found in (a handler registry)
  println(Registry.names().length)
  Registry.register("emit")
  Registry.register("fold")
  println(Registry.names().length)
  println(Registry.names().mkString(","))
  Registry.clear()
  println(Registry.names().length)

object Registry:
  var entries: List[String] = Nil
  def register(name: String): Unit = entries = name :: entries
  def clear(): Unit = entries = Nil
  def names(): List[String] = entries.reverse
```

Expected — the JVM lane is the oracle:

    0
    2
    2
    100
    50
    50
    0
    2
    emit,fold
    0
